1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,069
You're probably installing AI skills you never actually
run.

2
00:00:04,069 --> 00:00:10,240
So I moved one folder out of a YouTube skill pack, and it
broke somewhere I wasn't looking.

3
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:16,158
Eleven skills, six Python tools, five hundred thirty-five
lines of code.

4
00:00:16,158 --> 00:00:20,070
I installed all of it, and then I tried to break it.

5
00:00:20,070 --> 00:00:21,500
This is what broke.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,762
Here's the thing nobody says out loud.

7
00:00:24,762 --> 00:00:26,870
Most skill packs are prompts.

8
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:30,795
You read them, the model reads them, nothing executes.

9
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:36,828
This one is different. It ships six Python tools that are
supposed to actually run.

10
00:00:36,828 --> 00:00:42,280
And that matters, because a tool that silently fails is
worse than no tool.

11
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:46,360
So before trusting it, I audited it.

12
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:49,343
Every import in all six files.

13
00:00:49,343 --> 00:00:52,824
csv, json, os, re, statistics, sys.

14
00:00:52,824 --> 00:00:59,785
That's the whole list. No network. No subprocess. No eval.
No API key.

15
00:00:59,785 --> 00:01:04,260
Which means it runs anywhere, including here.

16
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:11,001
Then I broke it. I moved one folder, yt edit, out of the
pack. Seventeen checks.

17
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:15,526
Eight of them failed. And the error wasn't file not found.

18
00:01:15,526 --> 00:01:19,816
It was module not found error, no module named deadair.

19
00:01:19,816 --> 00:01:24,419
Because three of these tools import across sibling
folders.

20
00:01:24,419 --> 00:01:29,880
Move one, and two others break at runtime. Not at install.
At runtime.

21
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:32,939
That's the payoff, and here it is.

22
00:01:32,939 --> 00:01:36,551
An install that looks clean can still be broken.

23
00:01:36,551 --> 00:01:41,594
The only way to know is to run the tools, not to count the
folders.

24
00:01:41,594 --> 00:01:50,098
So I wrote one script that runs all six against generated
fixtures and asserts on their output. It's in the repo.

25
00:01:50,098 --> 00:01:53,259
npm run verify yt-skill. Seventeen checks.

26
00:01:53,259 --> 00:01:56,420
It fails loudly, which is the whole point.

27
00:01:56,920 --> 00:01:59,195
One tool genuinely surprised me.

28
00:01:59,195 --> 00:02:04,882
It ranks videos not by views, but by how far each beat its
own channel's median.

29
00:02:04,882 --> 00:02:09,289
A four hundred thousand view video on a huge channel?
Ignored.

30
00:02:09,289 --> 00:02:11,706
The same views on a small channel?

31
00:02:11,706 --> 00:02:15,829
Fourteen point seven eight times its median. Ranked first.

32
00:02:15,829 --> 00:02:21,160
That's a better idea detector than view count, and it's
seventy-four lines.

33
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:26,561
If you install one skill pack this week, run it before you
trust it.

34
00:02:26,561 --> 00:02:28,940
The verify script is in the repo.

